-{"id": "EXAMPLE-APP-001", "title": "[P1] Prove the versatility thesis: ship ONE application built directly on the memory store (a task board), with no separate backend", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T17:51:08Z", "reasoning": "Bane's framing 2026-11-04: DuckBrain's point is VERSATILITY - 'how close can you run the database and application server' - so that a user or an agent builds the feature they want directly on the store instead of chasing a separate app backend. He points at a peer project (punkrecords) that ships a task manager and kanban board as proof of the idea. DuckBrain today has the PRIMITIVES and no DEMONSTRATION: 'duckbrain tables declare' writes a SUPA-6 schema.json, the generic REST layer serves CRUD with filters/order/limit plus PATCH/DELETE by primary key, per-table grants are enforced on every verb, the SUPA-2 serializer audits every mutation, X-Durability is on every write, and SSE publishes the change feed - all verified on a scratch daemon 2026-11-04 (declare widgets -> insert -> ?qty=gte. -> PATCH ?pk=eq. -> DELETE ?pk=eq. -> audit row with contentHash). What does not exist is a single shipped example an outsider can run to see that building an app on the store is real. Without one, the versatility claim is a thesis, not a product.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) A task-board application lives in the repo (e.g. examples/taskboard/) whose ENTIRE persistence is DuckBrain: tables declared in schema.json (e.g. boards, columns, cards with ids, ordering, timestamps), all reads through GET /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table with query params, all writes through POST/PATCH/DELETE including primary-key addressing. No second datastore, no SQLite, no JSON file of its own - prove it with 'grep -r' in the app dir showing zero alternate persistence. (2) It runs against a scratch daemon from a documented one-command start, and a committed transcript/screenshot shows: create board -> create column -> create card -> MOVE a card between columns (ordering change visible) -> filter cards by assignee -> delete a card -> the resulting rows read back correctly. (3) Permissions are exercised, not decorative: a token WITHOUT write grant can read the board but gets 403 on POST/PATCH/DELETE, and the denial appears in the namespace audit with reason table_grant. (4) A README section states in plain steps how a user declares their OWN tables and gets the same CRUD+grants+audit surface - the point is that the pattern generalises, not that this app exists. (5) The change feed is shown being used: subscribe to the namespace, move a card in another shell, and the event arrives. (6) test-counts.json bumped in the same commit as any new suite; full suite green before landing.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T17:51:08Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "9c35865e60edca6b768cfd12c12fa8dbbdac84274df2bcb4e84e05ea94448360"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
+{"id": "EXAMPLE-APP-001", "title": "[P1] Prove the versatility thesis: ship ONE application built directly on the memory store (a task board), with no separate backend", "status": "in_progress", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T17:51:08Z", "reasoning": "Bane's framing 2026-11-04: DuckBrain's point is VERSATILITY - 'how close can you run the database and application server' - so that a user or an agent builds the feature they want directly on the store instead of chasing a separate app backend. He points at a peer project (punkrecords) that ships a task manager and kanban board as proof of the idea. DuckBrain today has the PRIMITIVES and no DEMONSTRATION: 'duckbrain tables declare' writes a SUPA-6 schema.json, the generic REST layer serves CRUD with filters/order/limit plus PATCH/DELETE by primary key, per-table grants are enforced on every verb, the SUPA-2 serializer audits every mutation, X-Durability is on every write, and SSE publishes the change feed - all verified on a scratch daemon 2026-11-04 (declare widgets -> insert -> ?qty=gte. -> PATCH ?pk=eq. -> DELETE ?pk=eq. -> audit row with contentHash). What does not exist is a single shipped example an outsider can run to see that building an app on the store is real. Without one, the versatility claim is a thesis, not a product.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) A task-board application lives in the repo (e.g. examples/taskboard/) whose ENTIRE persistence is DuckBrain: tables declared in schema.json (e.g. boards, columns, cards with ids, ordering, timestamps), all reads through GET /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table with query params, all writes through POST/PATCH/DELETE including primary-key addressing. No second datastore, no SQLite, no JSON file of its own - prove it with 'grep -r' in the app dir showing zero alternate persistence. (2) It runs against a scratch daemon from a documented one-command start, and a committed transcript/screenshot shows: create board -> create column -> create card -> MOVE a card between columns (ordering change visible) -> filter cards by assignee -> delete a card -> the resulting rows read back correctly. (3) Permissions are exercised, not decorative: a token WITHOUT write grant can read the board but gets 403 on POST/PATCH/DELETE, and the denial appears in the namespace audit with reason table_grant. (4) A README section states in plain steps how a user declares their OWN tables and gets the same CRUD+grants+audit surface - the point is that the pattern generalises, not that this app exists. (5) The change feed is shown being used: subscribe to the namespace, move a card in another shell, and the event arrives. (6) test-counts.json bumped in the same commit as any new suite; full suite green before landing.", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T19:03:01Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "9c35865e60edca6b768cfd12c12fa8dbbdac84274df2bcb4e84e05ea94448360"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "claimed by harden-loop 2026-11-05T19:03:01Z; worktree wt/EXAMPLE-APP-001"}
 {"id": "SEC-AUTHZ-001", "title": "[P1] 17 of 37 live API tokens are ADMIN because 'roles' is optional — the grant model is bypassed for half the fleet", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:06Z", "reasoning": "Found by the 2026-11-04 bakeoff while scoring our own tree (ours/duckbrain.json, row auth.legacy-token-admin-equivalence = WARNS_NEVER_REFUSES). src/auth/roles.ts:73-77: effectiveRoles() returns [\"admin\"] when principal.roles is undefined, documented as backward compatibility for the pre-SUPA-4 token shape. Measured against the live store (~/.duckbrain/auth.json, 37 keys): 17 have no roles field, and 37 of 37 carry no tableGrants. Consequence: (a) a token minted by any older tooling is a full admin on every namespace, silently — there is no signal at token creation and none at use; (b) the per-table grant layer SUPA-4 claims to enforce has never been exercised in production, so the only evidence it works is a scratch-daemon probe, not a deployment; (c) my own earlier report to the owner ('grants bite — verified: write-only grant GET 403, read-only DELETE 403') was true of the MECHANISM on a scratch daemon and misleading about the live system, which is the exact failure mode the boundary probe was supposed to prevent. Not a theoretical risk: an agent given a read-only key that was minted without a roles field can write and delete.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Make the default explicit and safe: a token with no roles field must not be admin — either default to a least-privilege role, or refuse to authenticate it with a clear error naming the token and the migration command. Prove with a test that a roles-less token cannot write to a namespace it was not granted, and that the failure names the reason. (2) Migrate the live population, do not just change the default: enumerate the 17 roles-less keys, assign each an intended role (ask the owner where intent is unknown — do NOT auto-grant admin to preserve the status quo), and re-count to 0 roles-less keys, printing before/after counts. (3) Exercise the grant layer for real at least once per role class: create one read-only token with explicit roles + explicit tableGrants and show GET 200 / PATCH 403 / DELETE 403 against a scratch daemon, with the denial visible in the namespace audit carrying reason table_grant. (4) Add a startup WARN (metrics + log) counting tokens without roles so the population cannot silently regrow; expose it as a number, not prose. (5) Update SUPA-4's row text: 'enforced' currently means 'the code can refuse', and it must also say how many live principals are actually subject to the rule.", "updated_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:06Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "2ce285ea317189b9c9d714fbfa56626de1fd6547c5c9eca091fad2c802e5386c"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": null}
 {"id": "DURABILITY-HONESTY-001", "title": "[P1] X-Durability reports the CONFIGURED mode, and the generic-table write path never fsyncs — the header overstates durability on the surface we sell as an app backend", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:07Z", "reasoning": "Found by the 2026-11-04 bakeoff (ours/duckbrain.json rows durability.x-durability-header-on-every-write = LOOKS_ENFORCED_IS_NOT and durability.fsync-before-ack-modes). src/storage/durability.ts:168-175: durabilityHeaderFor(ns, config) returns resolveWriteMode(ns, config) — a CONFIG LOOKUP, so the header states what the namespace is configured for, not what this write achieved. Separately, 'grep -rn fsync src/http/routes/tables.ts' returns nothing: the generic-table POST/PATCH/DELETE path acknowledges without any fsync, while the DDL coordinate path does fsync (src/serialization/ddl.ts:323-347, fsyncDir at :253). So a client that reads X-Durability: fsync from a table write is told something untrue on exactly the surface being pitched as an application backend. This also corrects my own report earlier today: I landed a row adding the header to table POST and described it as 'the durability header the write actually used' — it is the durability the namespace is CONFIGURED for.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Make the header truthful: either (a) have it report the mode the write actually achieved (the writer returns what it did, and a buffered write must never be able to claim fsync), or (b) rename/split it so configuration cannot be read as achievement (e.g. X-Durability-Mode: configured + a separate X-Durability-Achieved only where an fsync ran). Prove with a test that a namespace configured fsync but writing through a non-fsync path does NOT report an achieved fsync. (2) Close the gap it exposes: the generic-table write path must either fsync when the namespace is configured for it, or explicitly report buffered — and a test must fail if the achieved 