 {"id": "ADOPT-DECLARED-001", "title": "[P2] The declared-schema path is unused in production: 0 schema.json vs 949 legacy .table.json — and there is no HTTP/MCP way to declare a table", "status": "pending", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:15Z", "reasoning": "Found by the 2026-11-04 bakeoff, and it is the counterpart of the versatility thesis: the mechanism exists and nobody uses it. Measured: find namespaces -maxdepth 2 -name schema.json = 0; find namespaces -name '*.table.json' = 949. So every SUPA-6 feature demonstrated today (declare -> CRUD -> filters -> PATCH/DELETE by pk -> audit) runs on a code path no namespace has adopted, while the legacy declaration shape serves every real table. Second half: declaration is host-CLI only (duckbrain tables declare, src/cli/tables.ts); src/cli/http.ts mounts no POST route for it and the MCP surface has no verb for it, so an AGENT cannot declare a table — it needs a shell on the box. That is precisely the 'build the feature you want directly on the store' claim blocked at step one: the store can serve arbitrary app tables, but only a human with SSH can create one.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Give declaration a network surface: POST /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table (declaration body) that writes through the same validated path as the CLI, with authz that requires a write/admin grant on that namespace, returning the resulting declaration; prove with an end-to-end run on a scratch daemon where an AGENT-equivalent HTTP client declares a table and immediately POSTs+GETs a row. Do not add a second implementation — the route must call the same coordinator the CLI uses. (2) Migration or an honest decision: either migrate a pilot namespace to schema.json (prove row-for-row equivalence: same table list, same primary key, same columns, same rows read back) or record why the legacy shape remains canonical and what legacy costs (e.g. no change records on those inserts, per DURABILITY-HONESTY-001/SUPA-2). (3